"use strict";(self.webpackChunkdatabricks_monolith=self.webpackChunkdatabricks_monolith||[]).push([[70668],{270668:e=>{e.exports='# Machine Learning Best Practices\n\nThis skill provides guidance for developing, training, and evaluating machine learning models in Databricks notebooks.\n\n> **Note:** If there is any contradiction between the user\'s explicit preferences or instructions and the guidance in this skill, always defer to the user\'s preferences. The user\'s intent takes priority over these best practices.\n\n## General ML Development Approach\n\nWhen working on machine learning tasks, follow this systematic approach:\n\n1. **Exploratory Data Analysis (EDA)** - Understand your data through profiling, visualizations, and statistical analysis\n2. **Data Preprocessing** - Handle missing values, encode categorical features, and split data appropriately\n3. **Model Selection & Training** - Choose appropriate models based on task type (classification/regression/forecasting) and data characteristics\n4. **Hyperparameter Tuning** - Optimize model performance through systematic parameter search.\n5. **Evaluation** - Validate model performance using appropriate metrics\n6. **Experiment Tracking** - Log experiments, models, and results for reproducibility\n\nThese steps generally build upon each other. EDA and preprocessing are particularly important for model success and should not be skipped unless the user requests otherwise. MLflow experiment tracking is mandatory for all training runs.\n\nAlways restart python kernel using `dbutils.library.restartPython()` immediately after library installation. Install all the required libraries at the top of the notebook and rerun kernel right after it.\n\n## Exploratory Data Analysis\n\n### Overview\n\nPerform exploratory data analysis on the dataset before training the model. This critical step helps understand data characteristics, identify potential issues, and inform preprocessing and modeling decisions.\n\n### General EDA Steps\n\n- Do the following steps in code in the Notebook cell and feel free to execute SQL code if applicable\n- Perform exploratory data analysis on the dataset before training the model\n- Create a correllogram of features if applicable\n- Create at least two visualizations of correlations between input features and target variable for regression/classification tasks\n- Create a visualization for at least a portion of time series data for forecasting task\n- If applicable call describe(), shape(), head() and info() on the training DataFrame\n- Create a two-column table that in the first column lists features (or subset of them), and in the second column type of the feature (such as numeric float, numberic integer, categorical/nominal, categorical/ordinal, embedding, list, etc)\n\n### Classification-Specific EDA\n\n- For classification tasks visualize class frequencies. If any class falls below the K-aware threshold defined in [`classification.md`](classification.md) → "Class Imbalance Handling", follow that section before model selection.\n- For multi-class multi-label tasks compute and visualize correlation among labels. Compute and visualize label frequencies.\n\n## Data Preprocessing\n\n### Missing Values\n\n- Impute missing values for numerical columns with mean by default\n- Use `ColumnTransformer`, `SimpleImputer`, and `StandardScaler` from sklearn\n- Only use `dropna` as a final resort, prefer smarter methods when possible\n\n### Categorical Encoding\n\n- Convert each low-cardinality categorical column into multiple binary columns through one-hot encoding\n- For each input categorical column (string or numeric), the number of output columns is equal to the number of unique values in the input column\n- Use `category_encoders` library when possible\n\n### Data Splitting\n\nSplit the dataset into training, validation, and test sets.\n\nEnsure the training set is the largest portion of the data.\nOutput the selected split and a brief justification.\n\n## Feature Engineering (On Request Only)\n\n> **When to use:** Only apply declarative feature engineering when the user explicitly requests feature engineering using the Databricks Feature Engineering API or declarative feature pipelines. Do not proactively suggest or apply this approach for standard ML tasks.\n\nUse the Databricks Feature Engineering API to define features declaratively. Each feature is a structured object with a data source, a computation function, and an optional time window. Features are managed as Unity Catalog objects.\n\nKey workflow:\n\n1. Analyze data during EDA to identify entity types, timestamps, and lookup relationships\n2. Define `DeltaTableSource` per entity granularity (separate sources for different GROUP BY keys)\n3. Define features with `FeatureEngineeringClient.create_feature()`\n4. Create training set with `fe.create_training_set()` for point-in-time correct feature joining\n5. For holdout evaluation, use `DataFrameSource` with a union of train/test data as the feature source\n\nFor detailed API reference, code examples, known limitations, and common patterns, see [`feature-engineering.md`](feature-engineering.md).\n\n## Model Training\n\n- Use cross-validation when applicable to estimate model generalization performance and prevent overfitting\n- Train a single model suitable for the task to deliver results quickly. If the user requests it, consider evaluating additional model architectures for comparison\n- **By default, train a simple model with fixed hyperparameters** to deliver results quickly. Reserve systematic hyperparameter search (e.g., `optuna`, `RandomizedSearchCV`, `GridSearchCV`) for when the user explicitly requests optimization or when the task calls for a production-ready model. An efficient fixed-parameter model that finishes is more useful than an optimally-tuned model that takes significantly longer to produce. Hyperparameter search can be suggested as a follow up action.\n\n## Runtime Comment Decision\n\n1. Estimate workload internally from the user request and notebook context. Use signals such as row count, feature count, folds, candidates, trials, models, splits, and iterations. Only surface workload details to the user in step 2.\n\n2. If the workload is substantial: send a visible chat message before calling run or execution tools. State the concrete scale. Give a rough runtime estimate (upper bound should at most 2x of the lower bound).\n\n3. If the workload is NOT substantial: do NOT narrate the estimation at all. Simply update the cell and run it. Do NOT say anything about rows, fits, folds, timing, or compute cost — not even reassuringly. Do NOT surface workload estimation to the user. DO NOT make comments about the scale of the workload (# rows x # fits x # folds etc)\n\n### Example\n\n- Good: "Running this means 5 models \xd7 20 splits \xd7 5-fold CV = about 500 fits, so this is likely 5-10 minutes."\n- Bad: "This is a lightweight workload ... running it now."\n- Bad: "Running the cell — 50k rows \xd7 2 C values \xd7 2-fold CV is lightweight; should finish quickly."\n\n## Experiment Tracking\n\n### Overview\n\nUse experiment tracking to ensure reproducibility, compare model versions, and maintain a comprehensive history of your ML development process. Use `mlflow` library to log model training and evaluation results.\n\n### What to Track\n\n- Model hyperparameters\n- Training and validation metrics\n- Model artifacts\n- Training duration\n- Model signature\n\n### Best Practices\n\n- Start logging experiments from the beginning of the project\n- Use meaningful experiment and run names\n- Tag experiments for easy filtering and comparison\n- Log visualizations (plots, confusion matrices, ROC curves)\n- Record data preprocessing parameters\n- Track random seeds for reproducibility\n- Use MLflow\'s autologging when available\n- Organize experiments hierarchically for complex projects\n- Always include signatures when logging models\n\n## Task-specific instructions\n\nFor detailed information about different ML tasks:\n\n- **Feature Engineering** - [`feature-engineering.md`](feature-engineering.md)\n- **Classification** - [`classification.md`](classification.md)\n- **Regression** - [`regression.md`](regression.md)\n\n## Core Principle\n\nFollow the ML workflow as a guide: understand your data through EDA, preprocess it properly, select appropriate models based on task type and data characteristics, tune hyperparameters, evaluate thoroughly, and track experiments for reproducibility.\n'}}]);
